WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.482
Smart pointer choice is part of API
design.

2
00:00:02.562 --> 00:00:05.754
A function signature tells callers whether
the API wants owned data,

3
00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:09.247
borrowed data, shared ownership, mutable
access, thread-safe sharing,

4
00:00:09.327 --> 00:00:10.814
or a more specialized contract.

5
00:00:10.894 --> 00:00:12.655
If the signature uses a smart pointer,

6
00:00:12.735 --> 00:00:14.992
that pointer should express a real
requirement.

7
00:00:15.072 --> 00:00:17.880
Start with owned values or borrows when
they are sufficient.

8
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.060
Passing `&T`, `&mut T`,

9
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.939
or an owned value often communicates
intent more clearly than exposing reference

10
00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:24.615
counting or interior mutability.

11
00:00:24.695 --> 00:00:28.438
Adding `Rc<T>` or `Arc<T>` to an API can
force callers into a lifecycle model they

12
00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:29.619
did not otherwise need.

13
00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:31.626
`Cow` supports borrow-or-own behavior.

14
00:00:31.706 --> 00:00:35.504
It is useful when an API usually works
with borrowed data but occasionally needs to

15
00:00:35.584 --> 00:00:36.850
allocate or modify a value.

16
00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.839
The defensive value of `Cow` is that it
can avoid unnecessary allocation while still

17
00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:44.552
making the ownership transition explicit
when mutation or ownership becomes

18
00:00:44.632 --> 00:00:45.182
necessary.

19
00:00:45.262 --> 00:00:48.840
`Pin` appears in advanced designs where a
value must not move after it has been

20
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.507
pinned, such as some async and low-level
abstractions.

21
00:00:51.587 --> 00:00:54.835
At a conceptual level, `Pin` protects
stable-location assumptions.

22
00:00:54.915 --> 00:00:58.328
It should be introduced because the design
depends on those assumptions,

23
00:00:58.408 --> 00:01:01.106
not because it appears in examples or
feels more advanced.

24
00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:05.094
Review should ask whether the pointer
choice simplifies safety or hides confusion.

25
00:01:05.174 --> 00:01:07.266
Does the API actually need shared
ownership?

26
00:01:07.346 --> 00:01:08.832
Does mutation need to be shared?

27
00:01:08.912 --> 00:01:10.563
Can ownership stay with the caller?

28
00:01:10.643 --> 00:01:12.680
Could a borrow express the contract
better?

29
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.182
Are cleanup and lifetime expectations
documented?

30
00:01:15.262 --> 00:01:17.134
Good APIs make ownership easy to follow.

31
00:01:17.214 --> 00:01:21.067
They avoid exporting internal complexity
unless callers must participate in that

32
00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:21.753
complexity.

33
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:25.135
When smart pointers are necessary,
document the lifecycle, mutation,

34
00:01:25.215 --> 00:01:29.179
concurrency, and cleanup expectations so
callers and reviewers can reason about the

35
00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:30.580
contract without guessing.
